WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.210
Speaker 1: Well, one historic day in American
history.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:09.040
Yesterday, the January 6th committee of the
House of Representatives investigating the

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:16.539
Trump riots of 2021 has approved criminal
referrals for a former president.

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:18.840
No, it's not against Barack Obama.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.720
Oh, come on, guys.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.340
It's against failed former president Donald
Trump.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:31.750
NBC News reports January six Committee Approves
Criminal Referrals Against Trump for his role

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.780
in trying to overturn the 2020 election.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.730
The panel also issued a criminal referral
to the DOJ for a conservative attorney, John

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.730
Eastman.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.600
He's the genius who came up with the strategy.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.100
I don't even know what to call it.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:53.090
The idea for how former Vice President Mike
Pence could prevent the certification of the

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:54.090
2020 election.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:59.899
We are going to now hear from one of the members
of the committee, Jamie Raskin.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:00.899
Let's listen.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.620
Speaker 2: The first criminal statute we invoke
for referral, therefore, is Title 18, Section

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:16.880
1512 C, which makes it unlawful for anyone
to corruptly obstruct, influence or impede

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.340
any official proceeding of the United States
government.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.799
We believe that the evidence described by
my colleagues today and assembled throughout

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:34.689
our hearings warrants a criminal referral
of former President Donald J. Trump, John

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:38.790
Eastman and others for violations of this
statute.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.790
Wow.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:47.299
While the whole purpose and obvious effect
of Trump's scheme were to obstruct, influence

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:50.810
and impede this official proceeding.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:57.439
The central moment for the lawful transfer
of power in the United States.

00:01:57.439 --> 00:01:59.479
Second.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:03.860
We believe that there is more than sufficient
evidence to refer former President Donald

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:11.500
J. Trump, John Eastman and others for violating
Title 18, Section 371.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.270
This statute makes it a crime to conspire
to defraud the United States.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:24.730
In other words, to make an agreement to impair,
obstruct or defeat the lawful functions of

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:31.340
the United States government by deceitful
or dishonest means.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.970
Former President Trump did not engage in a
plan to defraud the United States acting alone.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:41.390
He entered into agreements formal and informal,
with several other individuals who assisted

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:42.790
him with his criminal objectives.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:43.900
Speaker 1: Also.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.330
Speaker 2: Our report describes in detail
the actions of numerous coconspirators who

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:56.890
agreed with and participated in Trump's plan
to impair, obstruct and defeat the certification

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.370
of President Biden's electoral victory.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:06.090
That said, the subcommittee does not attempts
to determine all of the potential participants

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:11.959
in this conspiracy, as our understanding of
the role of many individuals may be incomplete

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:16.200
even today because they refuse to answer our
questions.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.629
We trust that the Department of Justice will
be able to form a far more complete picture

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:23.870
through its own investigation.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:32.390
Third, we make a referral based on Title 18,
Section 1001, which makes it unlawful to knowingly

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:38.000
and willfully make materially false statements
to the federal government.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.010
And that is for.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.230
Speaker 1: Just in the interest of time.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.140
This was a very long hearing.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:49.099
Fourth is inciting, assisting or aiding comforting
an insurrection.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:52.849
So four different criminal referrals.

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:54.150
How did Trump react?

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.050
You could probably guess, but we're going
to look at that a little later.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:01.050
But let me address some of the emails that
I've received about this.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.530
This is a long overdue step, but that's all
it is.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.820
It is one small step.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.920
And there's a lot of doubt and cynicism in
the emails that I've been receiving from many

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.459
of you over the last 24 hours.

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:15.459
And I get it.

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:16.489
I do get it.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:21.590
We all want to see justice served against
these individuals.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:29.560
But it is a process and it's worth remembering
that the DOJ has to build the case.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.950
There is an astounding amount of evidence.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.800
They have to piece that case together.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.300
And we all know colloquially and folks, everyone
is innocent until proven guilty in a court

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.300
of law.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.300
This show is not a court of law.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.660
This is a place where we just speak frankly.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:52.540
We all know, as we read through the different
statutes that Jamie Raskin described there,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.340
we all know Trump did the stuff he's accused
of.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.350
We all know that colloquially speaking, he
did it.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:05.139
But the question starts to become, is there
the possibility of finding guilt beyond a

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:06.919
reasonable doubt here?

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:13.660
We all know and we watched enough to know
that colloquially speaking, of course, Donald

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.250
Trump did the things for which he is being
referred.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.690
And this includes all four of those items
that Jamie Raskin mentioned he was able to

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:21.690
incite.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:25.870
A mob stormed the Capitol on January six,
an attempt to disrupt the peaceful transition

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.949
of power and put the lives of members of Congress
and his own vice president at risk.

00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:30.949
All of it.

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:31.949
All of it.

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:32.949
All of it.

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:38.430
There are some who will argue that the criminal
referrals that we are seeing here are strictly

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.960
a political move.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.100
These aren't really law enforcement elements.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.410
This is just Democrats playing politics.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:50.889
And it may end up being that if it doesn't
go any further.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:52.910
But this is a necessary step.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:59.120
In other words, the committee did what it
had to do and it determined we have the evidence

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.880
for a criminal referral.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.949
It may stop there, but that's all that the
House committee can actually do.

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:08.190
Now, it is a question of what is Merrick Garland
willing to do?

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.340
As we spoke about yesterday and we talked
about the one word that is the reason why

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.680
presidents have not been indicted in the United
States.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:15.680
That word is tradition.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.490
And that's not a very strong reason not to
indict a president who did things no other

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.390
president has done.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:27.120
How could federal criminal charges affect
Trump's 2024 presidential run?

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:33.610
Becomes another political question as we wait
for answers to the legal questions in general.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.800
In like a normal world.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:41.100
Criminal charges against an individual while
they're running for president would affect

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.669
their reputation.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:44.520
It would affect their credibility.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.840
It would make it more difficult for them to
actually win.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:54.810
But we've already experienced with Donald
Trump in 2016 that seemingly nothing can actually

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.139
damage him.

00:06:56.139 --> 00:07:00.129
Enough with the Republican base to torpedo
his presidential campaign.

00:07:00.129 --> 00:07:01.129
2024.

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:05.370
It could be different if Trump were to be
convicted of a crime.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:10.509
Now we're facing many we're taking many hypothetical
steps into the future.

00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:12.480
He could face prison time.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.050
Yeah, that would make it difficult to hold
office.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:20.699
That would make it difficult to campaign even
if he was not convicted or imprisoned.

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:25.069
The fact that he would face criminal charges
could be a liability in a presidential campaign.

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:28.890
To the extent that it becomes a distraction,
it could raise questions about his judgment

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:29.960
or fitness for office.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:31.169
In a normal world.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:33.620
But this is the Republican electorate.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:39.050
This is the very same Republican electorate
who chose him in 2016 and who gleefully welcomed

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.210
him back in 2020 as their nominee.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:48.039
So any assumption that even criminal charges
or a conviction would torpedo Trump's campaign

00:07:48.039 --> 00:07:52.830
are possible, but potentially premature until
we actually see what happens.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:08:56.790
Let's now talk about Donald Trump's reaction

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:01.649
to this.

